1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Applications of machine learning have gotten a lot of traction in the last few years.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
There's a couple of big categories that have had wins.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,000
One is identifying pictures, the equivalent of finding cats on the internet,

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
and any problem that can be made to look like that.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
And the other is sequence-to-sequence translation.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
This can be speech to text or one language to another.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Most of the former are done with convolutional neural networks.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Most of the latter are done with recurrent neural networks,

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,000
particularly long short-term memory.

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
To give an example of how long short-term memory works,

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
we will consider the question of what's for dinner.

12
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Let's say for a minute that you are a very lucky apartment dweller,

13
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
and you have a flatmate who loves to cook dinner.

14
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Every night he cooks one of three things.

15
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Sushi, waffles, or pizza.

16
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
And you would like to be able to predict what you're going to have on a given night.

17
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
So you can plan the rest of your days eating accordingly.

18
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
In order to predict what you're going to have for dinner,

19
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
you set up a neural network.

20
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,000
The inputs to this neural network are a bunch of items like the day of the week,

21
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
the month of the year, whether or not your flatmate was in a late meeting,

22
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
variables that might reasonably affect what you're going to have for dinner.

23
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Now, if you're new to neural networks,

24
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:34,000
I highly recommend you take a minute and stop to watch the How Neural Networks Work tutorial.

25
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,000
There's a link down in the comments section.

26
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
If you'd rather not do that right now,

27
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
and you're still not familiar with neural networks,

28
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
you can think of them as a voting process.

29
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
And so in the neural network that you set up,

30
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:49,000
there's a complicated voting process,

31
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:53,000
and all of the inputs like day of the week and month of the year go into it,

32
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
and then you train it on your history of what you've had for dinner,

33
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
and you learn how to predict what's going to be for dinner tonight.

34
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
The trouble is that your network doesn't work very well.

35
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Despite carefully choosing your inputs and training it thoroughly,

36
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,000
you still can't get much better than chance predictions on dinner.

37
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,000
As is often the case with complicated machine learning problems,

38
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,000
it's useful to take a step back and just look at the data.

39
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,000
And when you do that, you notice a pattern.

40
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Your flat makes pizza, then sushi, then waffles, then pizza again in a cycle.

41
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
It doesn't depend on the day of the week or anything else.

42
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
It's in a regular cycle.

43
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
So knowing this, we can make a new neural network.

44
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,000
In our new one, the only inputs that matter are what we had for dinner yesterday.

45
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
So if we know if we had pizza for dinner yesterday, it'll be sushi tonight,

46
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,000
sushi yesterday, waffles tonight, and waffles yesterday, pizza tonight.

47
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:03,000
It becomes a very simple voting process, and it's right all the time,

48
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
because your flatmate is incredibly consistent.

49
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Now, if you happen to be gone on a given night, let's say yesterday you were out,

50
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
you don't know what was for dinner yesterday.

51
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
You can still predict what's going to be for dinner tonight

52
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:25,000
by thinking back two days ago, think what was for dinner then,

53
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
so what would be predicted for last night,

54
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,000
and then you can use that prediction in turn to make a prediction for tonight.

55
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:40,000
So we make use of not only our actual information from yesterday,

56
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
but also what our prediction was yesterday.

57
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,000
So at this point, it's helpful to take a little detour and talk about vectors.

58
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
A vector is just a fancy word for a list of numbers.

59
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
If I wanted to describe the weather to you for a given day,

60
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:00,000
I could say the high is 76 degrees Fahrenheit, the low is 43, the wind is 13 miles an hour,

61
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,000
there's going to be a quarter inch of rain, and the relative humidity is 83%.

62
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,000
That's all a vector is. The reason that it's useful is vectors,

63
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,000
lists of numbers are computers native language.

64
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,000
If you want to get something into a format that it's natural for a computer to compute,

65
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:22,000
to do operations on, to do statistical machine learning, lists of numbers are the way to go.

66
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Everything gets reduced to a list of numbers before it goes through an algorithm.

67
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,000
We can also have a vector for statements like, it's Tuesday.

68
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
In order to encode this kind of information,

69
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,000
what we do is we make a list of all the possible values it could have,

70
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,000
in this case, all the days of the week, and we assign a number to each.

71
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:49,000
And then we go through and set them all equal to zero except for the one that is true right now.

72
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
This format is called one-hot encoding,

73
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:58,000
and it's very common to see a long vector of zeros with just one element being one.

74
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:04,000
It seems inefficient, but for a computer, this is a lot easier way to ingest that information.

75
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:10,000
So we can make a one-hot vector for our prediction for dinner tonight.

76
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
We set everything equal to zero except for the dinner item that we predict.

77
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,000
So in this case, we'll be predicting sushi.

78
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Now we can group together our inputs and outputs into vectors, separate lists of numbers,

79
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
and it becomes a useful shorthand for describing this neural network.

80
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:38,000
So we can have our dinner yesterday vector, our predictions for yesterday vector,

81
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,000
and our prediction for today vector.

82
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:48,000
And the neural network is just connections between every element in each of those input vectors

83
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
to every element in the output vector.

84
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:57,000
And to complete our picture, we can show how the prediction for today will get recycled.

85
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:02,000
The dotted line there means hold on to it for a day and then reuse it tomorrow.

86
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:06,000
And it becomes our yesterday's predictions tomorrow.

87
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Now we can see how if we were lacking some information, let's say we were out of town for two weeks,

88
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,000
we can still make a good guess about what's going to be for dinner tonight.

89
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:23,000
We just ignore the new information part and we can unwrap or unwind this vector in time

90
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:29,000
until we do have some information to base it on and then just play it forward.

91
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
And when it's unwrapped, it looks like this.

92
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:37,000
And we can go back as far as we need to and see what was for dinner and then just trace it forward

93
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:43,000
and play out our menu over the last two weeks until we find out what's for dinner tonight.

94
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:47,000
So this is a nice simple example that showed recurrent neural networks.

95
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Now to show how they don't meet all of our needs, we're going to write a children's book.

96
00:06:52,000 --> 00:07:05,000
It'll have sentences of the format Doug saw Jane, period, Jane saw spot, period, spot saw Doug, period, and so on.

97
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:13,000
So our dictionary is small, just the words Doug, Jane, spot, saw, and a period.

98
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:19,000
And the task of the neural network is to put these together in the right order to make a good children's book.

99
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:26,000
So to do this, we replace our food vectors with our dictionary vectors.

100
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:31,000
Here again, it's just a list of numbers representing each of the words.

101
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:43,000
So for instance, if Doug was the most recent word that I saw, my new information vector would be all zeros except for a one in the Doug position.

102
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:49,000
And we similarly can represent our predictions and our predictions from yesterday.

103
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Now, after training this neural network and teaching it what to do, we would expect to see certain patterns.

104
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:08,000
For instance, anytime a name comes up, Jane, Doug, or spot, we would expect that to vote heavily for the word saw or for a period.

105
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:14,000
Because those are the two words in our dictionary that can follow a name.

106
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:25,000
Similarly, if we had predicted a name on the previous time step, we would expect those to vote also for the word saw or for a period.

107
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:33,000
And then by a similar method, anytime we come across the word saw or a period, we know that a name has to come after that.

108
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:43,000
So it will learn to vote very strongly for a name, Jane, Doug, or spot.

109
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:49,000
So in this form, in this formulation, we have a recurrent neural network.

110
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:58,000
For simplicity, I'll take the vectors and the weights and collapse them down to that little symbol with the dots and the arrows, the dots and the lines connecting them.

111
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,000
And there's one more symbol we haven't talked about yet.

112
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:06,000
This is a squashing function, and it just helps the network to behave.

113
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:14,000
How it works is you take all of your votes coming out and you subject them to this squashing function.

114
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:27,000
For instance, if something received a total vote of 0.5, you draw a vertical line up where it crosses the function, you draw a horizontal line over to the y-axis, and there's your squashed version out.

115
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:38,000
For small numbers, the squashed version is pretty close to the original version, but as your number gets larger, the number that comes out is closer and closer to 1.

116
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:44,000
And similarly, if you put in a big negative number, then what you'll get out will be very close to minus 1.

117
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:50,000
No matter what you put in, what comes out is between minus 1 and 1.

118
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:59,000
So this is really helpful when you have a loop like this, where the same values get processed again and again, day after day.

119
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:07,000
It is possible, you can imagine if in the course of that processing, say something got voted for twice, it got multiplied by 2.

120
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:15,000
In that case, it would get twice as big every time and very soon blow up to be astronomical.

121
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:24,000
By ensuring that it's always less than 1, but more than minus 1, you can multiply it as many times as you want.

122
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
You can go through that loop and it won't explode.

123
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:33,000
In a feedback loop, this is an example of negative feedback or attenuating feedback.

124
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:38,000
So you may have noticed our neural network in its current state is subject to some mistakes.

125
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:45,000
We could get a sentence, for instance, of the form, dug, saw, dug, period.

126
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:53,000
Because dug strongly votes for the word saw, which in turn strongly votes for unnamed, any name, which could be dug.

127
00:10:53,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Similarly, we could get something like dug, saw Jane, saw Spot, saw Doug.

128
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:08,000
Because each of our predictions only looks back one time step, it has very short term memory,

129
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:15,000
then it doesn't use the information from further back and it's subject to these types of mistakes.

130
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:23,000
In order to overcome this, we take our recurrent neural network and we expand it and we add some more pieces to it.

131
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:28,000
The critical part that we add to the middle here is memory.

132
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:34,000
We want to be able to remember what happened many time steps ago.

133
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:40,000
So in order to explain how this works, I'll have to describe a few new symbols that we've introduced here.

134
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:45,000
One is another squashing function, this one with a flat bottom.

135
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:51,000
One is an X in a circle and one is a cross in a circle.

136
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:56,000
So the cross in a circle is element by element addition.

137
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:02,000
The way it works is you start with two vectors of equal size and you go down each one,

138
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:07,000
you add the first element of one vector to the first element of another vector,

139
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:12,000
and then the total goes into the first element of the output vector.

140
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:15,000
So 3 plus 6 equals 9.

141
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:19,000
Then you go to the next element, 4 plus 7 equals 11.

142
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:24,000
And so your output vector is the same size of each of your input vectors.

143
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:32,000
Just a list of numbers, same length, but it's the sum element by element of the two.

144
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:40,000
And very closely related to this, you've probably guessed, the X in the circle is element by element multiplication.

145
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:48,000
It's just like addition, except instead of adding, you multiply, for instance, 3 times 6 gives you a first element of 18.

146
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:51,000
4 times 7 gets you 28.

147
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:58,000
Again, the output vector is the same size of each of the input vectors.

148
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:04,000
Now, element-wise multiplication lets you do something pretty cool.

149
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:09,000
Imagine that you have a signal, and it's like a bunch of pipes,

150
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:13,000
and they have a certain amount of water trying to flow down them.

151
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:18,000
In this case, we'll just assign the number to that, a 0.8. It's like a signal.

152
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:25,000
Now, on each of those pipes, we have a faucet, and we can open it all the way, close it all the way,

153
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:30,000
or keep it somewhere in the middle to either let that signal come through or block it.

154
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:37,000
So in this case, an open gate, an open faucet, would be a 1, and a closed faucet would be a 0.

155
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:45,000
And the way this works with element-wise multiplication, we get 0.8 times 1 equals 0.8.

156
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:54,000
That signal passed right through into the output vector, but the last element, 0.8 times 0 equals 0.

157
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:58,000
That signal, the original signal, was effectively blocked.

158
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:06,000
And then with the gating value of 0.5, the signal was passed through, but it's smaller. It's attenuated.

159
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:14,000
So gating lets us control what passes through and what gets blocked, which is really useful.

160
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:22,000
Now, in order to do gating, it's nice to have a value that you know is always between 0 and 1.

161
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:28,000
So we introduce another squashing function. This will represent with a circle with a flat bottom,

162
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:35,000
and it's called the logistic function. It's very similar to the other squashing function, the hyperbolic tangent,

163
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:40,000
except that it just goes between 0 and 1 instead of minus 1 and 1.

164
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:46,000
Now, when we introduce all of these together, what we get?

165
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:51,000
We still have the combination of our previous predictions and our new information.

166
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:56,000
Those vectors get passed, and we make predictions based on them.

167
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:04,000
Those predictions get passed through, but the other thing that happens is a copy of those predictions

168
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:10,000
is held on to for the next time step, the next pass through the network.

169
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:17,000
And some of them, here's a gate right here, some of them are forgotten, some of them are remembered,

170
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:21,000
the ones that are remembered are added back into the prediction.

171
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:28,000
So now we have not just predictions plus the memories that we've accumulated

172
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
and that we haven't chosen to forget yet.

173
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Now, there's an entirely separate neural network here that learns when to forget what.

174
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:45,000
Based on what we're seeing right now, what do we want to remember, what do we want to forget?

175
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:52,000
So you can see this is powerful. This will let us hold on to things for as long as we want.

176
00:15:52,000 --> 00:16:00,000
Now, you've probably noticed, though, when we are combining our predictions with our memories,

177
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:06,000
we may not necessarily want to release all of those memories out as new predictions each time.

178
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:13,000
So we want a little filter to keep our memories inside and let our predictions get out.

179
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:17,000
And that's, we add another gate for that to do selection.

180
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:21,000
It has its own neural network, so its own voting process,

181
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:28,000
so that our new information and our previous predictions can be used to vote on what all the gates should be,

182
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:33,000
what should be kept internal, and what should be released as a prediction.

183
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:36,000
We've also introduced another squashing function here.

184
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:42,000
Since we do an addition here, it's possible that things could become greater than one or smaller than minus one,

185
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:47,000
so we just squash it to be careful to make sure it never gets out of control.

186
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:53,000
And now, when we bring in new predictions, we make a lot of possibilities,

187
00:16:53,000 --> 00:17:00,000
and then we collect those with memory over time, and of all of those possible predictions,

188
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:07,000
at each time step, we select just a few to release as the prediction for that moment.

189
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:17,000
Each of these things, when to forget and when to let things out of our memory, are learned by their own neural networks.

190
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:25,000
And the only other piece we need to add to complete our picture here is yet another set of gates.

191
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:32,000
This lets us actually ignore possible predictions, possibilities as they come in.

192
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
This is an attention mechanism.

193
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:43,000
It lets things that aren't immediately relevant be set aside so they don't cloud the predictions in memory going forward.

194
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:52,000
It has its own neural network and its own logistic squashing function and its own gating activity right here.

195
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:57,000
Now, long short-term memory has a lot of pieces, a lot of bits that work together,

196
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:00,000
and it's a little much to wrap your head around it all at once,

197
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:07,000
so what we'll do is take a very simple example and step through it just to illustrate how a couple of these pieces work.

198
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:10,000
It's admittedly an overly simplistic example,

199
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
and feel free to poke holes at it later.

200
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:17,000
When you get to that point, then you know you're ready to move on to the next level of material.

201
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:22,000
So, we are now in the process of writing our children's book.

202
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:31,000
And for the purposes of demonstration, we'll assume that this LSTM has been trained on our children's books,

203
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:34,000
examples that we want to mimic.

204
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:40,000
And all of the appropriate votes and weights in those neural networks have been learned.

205
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:43,000
Now, we'll show it in action.

206
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:48,000
So, so far, our story so far is Jane saw spot, period.

207
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:54,000
Doug, so Doug is the most recent word that's occurred in our story.

208
00:18:54,000 --> 00:19:04,000
And also, not surprisingly for this time step, the names Doug, Jane, and Spot were all predicted as viable options.

209
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:07,000
This makes sense. We had just wrapped up a sentence with a period.

210
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:11,000
The new sentence can start with any name, so these are all great predictions.

211
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:16,000
So, we have our new information, which is the word Doug.

212
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:20,000
We have our recent prediction, which is Doug, Jane, and Spot.

213
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:26,000
And we pass these two vectors together to all four of our neural networks,

214
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:35,000
which are learning to make predictions, to do it ignoring, to do forgetting, and to do selection.

215
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:41,000
So, the first one of these makes some predictions.

216
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:51,000
Given that the word Doug just occurred, this is learned that the word saw is a great guess to make for a next word.

217
00:19:51,000 --> 00:20:00,000
But it's also learned that having seen the word Doug, that it should not see the word Doug again very soon.

218
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
Seeing the word Doug at the beginning of a sentence.

219
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:07,000
So, it makes a positive prediction for saw and a negative prediction for Doug.

220
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:10,000
It says, I do not expect to see Doug in the near future.

221
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:13,000
So, that's why Doug is in black.

222
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:18,000
So, this example is so simple, we don't need to focus on attention or ignoring.

223
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
So, we'll skip over it for now.

224
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:25,000
And this prediction of saw, not Doug, is passed forward.

225
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:30,000
And again, for the purposes of simplicity, let's say there's no memory at the moment.

226
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:34,000
So, saw and Doug get passed forward.

227
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:47,000
And then, the selection mechanism here has learned that when the most recent word was a name, then what comes next is either going to be the word saw or a period.

228
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:50,000
So, it blocks any other names from coming out.

229
00:20:50,000 --> 00:21:01,000
So, the fact that there's a vote for not Doug gets blocked here and the word saw gets sent out as the prediction for the next time step.

230
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:05,000
So, we take a step forward in time now.

231
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:09,000
The word saw is our most recent word and our most recent prediction.

232
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:14,000
They get passed forward to all of these neural networks and we get a new set of predictions.

233
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:24,000
Because the word saw just occurred, we now predict that the words Doug, Jane, or Spot might come next.

234
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:30,000
We'll pass over ignoring and attention in this example and we'll take those predictions forward.

235
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:44,000
Now, the other thing that happened is our previous set of possibilities, the word saw and not Doug, that we were maintaining internally, get passed to a forgetting gate.

236
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:52,000
Now, the forgetting gate says, hey, my last word that occurred was the word saw.

237
00:21:52,000 --> 00:22:03,000
Based on my past experience, then I can forget about, you know, I know that it occurred, I can forget that it happened, but I want to keep any predictions having to do with names.

238
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:17,000
So, it forgets saw, holds on to the vote for not Doug, and now at this element by element addition, we have a positive vote for Doug, a negative vote for Doug, and so they cancel each other out.

239
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:22,000
So, now we just have votes for Jane and Spot.

240
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
Those get passed forward.

241
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:36,000
Our selection gate, it knows that the word saw just occurred and based on experience, a name will happen next, and so it passes through these predictions for names.

242
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:43,000
And for the next time step then, we get predictions of only Jane and Spot, not Doug.

243
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:51,000
This avoids the Doug saw, Doug period type of error and the other errors that we saw.

244
00:22:51,000 --> 00:23:06,000
What this shows is that long short term memory can look back to three many time steps and use that information to make good predictions about what's going to happen next.

245
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:15,000
Now, to be fair to vanilla recurrent neural networks, they can actually look back several time steps as well, but not very many.

246
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:23,000
LSTM can look back many time steps and has shown that successfully.

247
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:29,000
This is really useful in some surprisingly practical applications.

248
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:37,000
If I have text in one language and I want to translate it to text to another language, LSTMs work very well.

249
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:54,000
Even though translation is not a word to word process, it's a phrase to phrase or even in some cases a sentence to sentence process, LSTMs are able to represent those grammar structures that are specific to each language.

250
00:23:54,000 --> 00:24:09,000
And what it looks like is that they find the higher level idea and translate it from one mode of expression to another, just using the bits and pieces that we just walked through.

251
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:14,000
Another thing that they do well is translating speech to text.

252
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:18,000
Speech is just some signals that vary in time.

253
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:33,000
It takes them and uses that then to predict what text, what word is being spoken, and it can use the history, the recent history of words to make a better guess for what's going to come next.

254
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:42,000
LSTMs are a great fit for any information that's embedded in time, audio, video.

255
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:45,000
My favorite application of all of course is robotics.

256
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:59,000
Robotics is nothing more than an agent taking in information from a set of sensors and then based on that information making a decision and carrying out an action.

257
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:11,000
It's inherently sequential and actions taken now can influence what is sensed and what should be done many time steps down the line.

258
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:20,000
If you're curious what LSTMs look like in math, this is it. This is lifted straight from the Wikipedia page.

259
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:31,000
I won't step through it, but it's encouraging that something that looks so complex, expressed mathematically, actually makes a fairly straightforward picture and story.

260
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:36,000
And if you'd like to dig into it more, I encourage you to go to the Wikipedia page.

261
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:46,000
Also, there are a collection of really good tutorials and discussions, other ways of explaining LSTMs that you may find helpful as well.

262
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:55,000
I'd also strongly encourage you to visit Andre Karpathy's blog post showing examples of what LSTMs can do in text.

263
00:25:55,000 --> 00:26:06,000
And if you haven't seen it yet, take a look at the video on how neural networks work to get some more details on exactly how you'd go about implementing something like this in code.

264
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:12,000
Thanks for tuning in. I wish you a lot of luck on your next project, Building with Recurrent Neural Networks.

